{"name":"Thrush","hex":"#936b4f","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Thrush","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A surname transferred from the nickname."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Thrush"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thrush (bird)","blocks":[{"text":"The thrushes are a passerine bird family, Turdidae, with a worldwide distribution. The family was once much larger before biologists reclassified the former subfamily Saxicolinae, which includes the chats and European robins, as Old World flycatchers. Thrushes are small to medium-sized ground living birds that feed on insects, other invertebrates, and fruit. Some unrelated species around the world have been named after thrushes due to their similarity to birds in this family."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Psophocichla_litsitsirupa_%28Etosha%29.jpg/330px-Psophocichla_litsitsirupa_%28Etosha%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrush_(bird)"},{"title":"List of thrush species","blocks":[{"text":"Thrushes and allies form the passerine bird family Turdidae. In 2024, the International Ornithological Committee (IOC) accepted 193 species of thrushes, with one more added in 2025. Of these, 105 are in the genus Turdus and the rest are distributed among 15 other genera. This list includes five extinct species that are marked (X)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Abhishek532_Grandala2.jpg/330px-Abhishek532_Grandala2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_thrush_species"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Vogels_door_Jan_van_Oort_%2802%29.jpg/500px-Vogels_door_Jan_van_Oort_%2802%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thum